              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Camille Saint-Saëns</strong><br>\nCamille Saint-Saëns was a French composer, born in Paris in 1835. He was a musical prodigy, displaying perfect pitch at the age of two. He began piano lessons with his great-aunt at the age of two and a half and wrote his first piece for piano at the age of three. His concert debut, at ten years old, was met with amazement, and by that time, he had mastered the piano to such an extent that he offered to play any of Beethoven's 32 piano sonatas from memory as an encore.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Saint-Saëns's Musical Education</strong><br>\nSaint-Saëns's formal musical education was at the Paris Conservatoire, where he studied organ and composition. His talents were recognized early, and he was only 16 when he wrote his first symphony. His education was thorough, providing him with a strong foundation in classical forms, which would be evident in his later compositions. He was highly knowledgeable about musical history, which influenced his style that bridged the old and the new.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Professional Career and Compositions</strong><br>\nAfter graduating, Saint-Saëns held various positions as a church organist, including a prestigious post at La Madeleine in Paris. His reputation as a composer grew with his Introduction and Rondo Capriccioso and the famous opera \"Samson and Delilah.\" He also composed symphonies, concertos, and chamber works that gained considerable acclaim. Saint-Saëns was notable for his ability to write in many different musical genres.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
