      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Innovation and Musical Influence</strong><br>\nSaint-Saëns was one of the first composers to write film music, composing the score for the silent film \"The Assassination of the Duke of Guise\" in 1908. He was also an innovator in symphonic poems, a form which he used to introduce his own compositions. His most famous symphonic poem, \"Danse Macabre,\" became a staple in the classical music repertoire and showcased his innovative orchestration techniques.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Saint-Saëns's Role in French Music</strong><br>\nAs an ardent supporter of French music, Saint-Saëns co-founded the National Society of Music to promote French compositions. He was an advocate for the new French music against the dominant Germanic traditions of the time. His influence extended to his students and contemporaries, and he became a national figure in French cultural life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Saint-Saëns as a Pianist and Organist</strong><br>\nSaint-Saëns was not only a composer but also an outstanding pianist and organist. His skills were so remarkable that famous composers like Franz Liszt praised him, calling him the greatest organist in the world. These skills were not just performance-based but also extended to teaching, with Saint-Saëns serving as a revered instructor for future generations of musicians.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
