            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Travels and Zoological Interests</strong><br>\nLater in life, Saint-Saëns traveled extensively. He was not only interested in music but also in many other fields, including archaeology, philosophy, and zoology. He wrote scholarly articles on various subjects and composed the \"Carnival of the Animals,\" a humorous musical suite that has become one of his most popular works, drawing on his interest in the animal kingdom and natural history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Character</strong><br>\nSaint-Saëns was known for his sharp intellect and private nature. Although he had a wide circle of friends and acquaintances in the arts, he kept his personal life out of the public eye. His marriage ended in tragedy with the deaths of his two sons, after which he separated from his wife and led a solitary life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nCamille Saint-Saëns left behind a vast body of work when he passed away in 1921. He had a significant impact on music and inspired many future composers with his stylistic versatility and strong understanding of classical and romantic traditions. His death marked the end of an era but also the beginning of a lasting legacy that continued to influence music long after his passing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
